dNethack does three main things: it greatly increases the number of artifac=
ts, implements new monsters, and re-organizes and expands the dungeon. A fe=
w changes have also been made to existing classes and items. Most new artif=
acts have custom effects and/or invocations. Most new monsters have code su=
pport beyond simply an entry in the monster list. Dungeon changes include: =
multi-level alignment =93quest=94 branches, additional side branches off th=
e main dungeon, shortened gehennom, demon lairs randomly drawn from a small=
 pool, and fewer straight-up maze levels. Characters of any alignment may e=
nter any alignment =93quest.=94 To complete the game, you must explore one =
quest thoroughly or multiple quests partially. Quests may be entered at any=
 time, and start easy, although the difficulty quickly increases beyond wha=
t a beginning character can handle.

Overall, dnethack is more difficult than vanilla nethack, as there are more=
 ways to get into trouble as a late-game character, and several ways to go =
from ascension-ready character to tombstone in just a few rounds. The desig=
n goal is for all such threats to be counterable, so that the late game is =
more difficult and dangerous but not randomly lethal, but you will probably=
 die several times while learning things the hard way. This seems par for t=
he course in nethack.


